{"name":"Craving Cobalt","hex":"#016496","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"craving","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A strong desire; yearning."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of crave"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/craving"},{"term":"cobalt","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A chemical element (symbol Co) with an atomic number of 27: a hard, lustrous, silver-gray metal.","Cobalt blue."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cobalt"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cobalt","blocks":[{"text":"Cobalt is a chemical element; it has symbol Co and atomic number 27. As with nickel, cobalt is found in the Earth's crust only in a chemically combined form, save for small deposits found in alloys of natural meteoric iron. The free element, produced by reductive smelting, is a hard, lustrous, somewhat brittle, gray metal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Kobalt_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/330px-Kobalt_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cobalt"},{"title":"Cobalt blue","blocks":[{"text":"Cobalt blue is a blue pigment consisting principally of cobalt(II) aluminate, CoAl2O4, made by sintering cobalt(II) oxide with aluminium(III) oxide (alumina) at high temperatures. It is used as a ceramic color, in automotive and industrial coatings, in scientific and medical imaging, and as a fine-art pigment. Its thermostability, chemical inertness, and lightfastness make it suitable for applications requiring a durable blue color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Cobalt_Blue.JPG/330px-Cobalt_Blue.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cobalt_blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Kobalt_13g.jpg/500px-Kobal